how is it that thousands potentially  tens of thousands of people  were unnecessarily exposed to covid19 it  is  a staggering story and at the heart of  it is this a computer error  message that might seem improbable but  believe me  this is all true and it turns out that  you know if we look back at the  architecture of the testing system  we can see what's gone wrong so these  boxes here you don't really need to know  about what's  what's in them but all you need to know  is that these green boxes are millions  of people being tested for covet across  the country the information goes through  to the blue boxes that's the contact  traces so ensuring  that any of those outbreaks are clamped  down on and in the middle of it  is public health england so it's kind of  like the gateway for all of this data  i'll just do a slightly more simplified  version of that so public health england  in the middle  test results go through into test and  trace and  that's been working relatively well so  far and partly because they've got an  existing database to deal with this  stuff so  you've got those kind of nhs labs around  the country most of the ones in  hospitals  they automatically pump all of these  results through to public health england  that automatically goes through  to test and trace that's how it works  but here's the thing a lot of tests are  not done by these  nhs hospital labs they're done these  days by something called pillar two now  these are the new labs those centralized  ones  run by serco deloitte and so on  lighthouse labs  and they don't have a direct route  through to this database  so here's where the problem comes in  they've had to jerry rig the system  instead  they're kind of uploading csv is a type  of spreadsheet  through to a computer in public health  england running excel  which then pumps them out down that  highway  but i should say at this stage you  really should never do that you should  never use excel as a database management  tool  and we're about to understand why  because in public health england it  turns out the version of excel they were  running  is old it's an old version of excel and  it has this limit on how much data you  can actually put  into it a 65 000 row limit sounds like a  lot but actually think about  it the cases have been getting more and  more over the course of the last  few weeks and it turns out that it broke  through  that limit they got the computer error  message and as a result  thousands of cases weren't pushed  through to test and trace until they  discovered about this  uh just on friday and it does somewhat  change our picture of this disease  in the country this is what we thought  was happening to cases uh over the  course of the last few months and now  we've had this big influx  of new data and a lot of that should be  said as a backlog from previous days but  it looks like a scary chart though  but it's worth asking does it mean that  that's a patrick valance illustration we  heard about a couple of weeks ago  is more likely to happen you remember  the one the one that showed the cases  might go up and up and up if we didn't  clamp down on them well here's what's  happened uh as far as we knew it before  the weekend so this is what happened to  cases  uh before the weekend now we've got  revised numbers you can see the line is  a bit steeper  but it's still nowhere near the kind of  doubling every seven days that we saw  into bacteria balances illustration it's  more like doubling every 11 to 12 days  even so though even if this doesn't  radically change the picture  of this disease in the uk it is still  striking that a computer error of all  things  meant that thousands of people have been  exposed to this disease
 

